A||||3||Gold-86SE||87 2.5 Duke Stalling (Can TheOgre Help Me?) Kinda Long, Sorry||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||05-05-2001||09:32 AM||||<BR>Guys, first sorry for the length, but I believe all of this description is relevant to determining my problem.<P>I have been working on this duck for 4 weeks and am trying to get it back to a young lady, but I can't until I have solved this stalling/dying issue.<P>First, I did a search and tried the couple of things that I found relevant.  Disconnecting the TCC solenoid and examining the egr valve.<P>Now, I am currently running w/o the TCC plug connected to the torque convertor solenoid, but the Fiero is still dying. So, I pulled off the egr valve and found that the prior mechanic had used RTV as a partial sealant. (now, it is my understanding that rtv is not supposed to be used on the egr, unless someone informs me differently.) I cleaned up the mating surfaces on the egr and used an old gasket when I remounted the egr. Additionally, the egr seems to be in okay shape, because the diaphragm does not stick.<P>The duck has 175k miles, and I have just done a major tune up (plugs, wires, pcv, air cleaner, breather filter, fuel filter, oil and filter change) had to replace the oil pan gasket, timing cover gasket and seal, and transmission pan gasket and filter change.<P>Furthermore, I don't know how old the fuel pump is, and I am almost thinking that it may not be delivering enough fuel after a hard stop is engaged.<P>Here is how it dies.<BR>Take it on the road and accelerate to around 40mph, the automatic reaches third gear, I do a hard stop (imitating an emergency maneuver) and the engine stalls, but is fairly easy to restart.<P>I can also have it die in the garage by power braking.  While power braking, I will rev it for about 15 to 20 seconds and let off the gas and it dies.  Additionally, if I kill the engine in this manner, it is difficult to restart the engine and it appears that there is black exhaust (running rich?).<P>Furthermore, it will start and run just fine while setting in the garage, at idle.  But it appears that as soon as I place a load on the engine, it can't handle the slow down back to idle.<P>So what gives? I am at a loss and would like some advice.  Could this be a plugged catalytic converter?<P>Sorry, edited for a note(s).<BR>I am not receiving any ecm codes, and yes, there is a light blub in the ses light.<P>Additional, on the tune up: new serpentine belt, new 02 sensor, replaced exhaust manifold and exhaust manifold gasket.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 05-05-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||05-05-2001||03:43 PM||||<BR>bump.<P>i need some help.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-05-2001||05:39 PM||||First off.... Silicon sealant may have ruined the O2 sensor. Only "Sensor Safe" sealants must be used in these engines. (You can get away with regular sealant only for things like valve cover, oil pan, water pump... stuff that does not come in contact with air/fuel moving thru the engine. Even then I prefer to avoid anything not listed as sensor safe.)<P>On the hard stop problem.... I beleive there is a baffle in the gas tank that is suposed to prevent the pump getting starved while braking. If that's come apart then well.....<P>Also check the TBI Very carefully. If the air cleaner has whacked the trunk wall the TBI gasket may be shot. That will cause a vac leak.<P>I'd still replace the EGR. If the silcon got inside then it could be sticking open.<P>There's notes about all this in my cave...<P>As for power braking.... That will always cause problems of some kind. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||05-06-2001||08:37 AM||||Hey Gold!<BR>Sounds like a vacuume leak in the brake system...<BR>The boost chamber may be leaking, and when you apply the brakes, it dies?<BR>Or it is the check valve?<BR>Good luck!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
